  "method": "The 'Per Capita' column of the ten-year table in two ACFRs: FY2007-FY2016: Comptroller's CAFR for Fiscal 2016, 'Ratios of Outstanding Debt by Type-Ten Year Trend', printed page 378 (PDF page 407), https://comptroller.nyc.gov/wp-content/uploads/documents/CAFR2016.pdf; FY2017-FY2026: Comptroller's ACFR for Fiscal 2026, 'Ratios of Outstanding Debt by Type-Ten Year Trend', printed page 463 (PDF page 497), https://comptroller.nyc.gov/wp-content/uploads/documents/ACFR-2026.pdf. Per capita is the Comptroller's figure: total primary government debt divided by the prior year's city population (table footnote 4). Each fiscal year's nominal figure is converted to 2025 dollars by multiplying by the ratio of the 2025 annual-average New York-Newark-Jersey City CPI-U (BLS CUURS12ASA0, not seasonally adjusted, 345.511) to the average of that index over the fiscal year (July of the prior year through June), from the BLS public API. FY2026 averages 11 months because BLS published no value for October 2025 (Data unavailable due to the 2025 lapse in appropriations). The nominal figures are in nominal_series and the fiscal-year index values in deflator.",
  "caveats": [
    "Lease obligations rose sharply in FY2022 when the City implemented GASB Statement 87 (ACFR FY2026 table footnote 5), so much of the FY2021-FY2022 jump (from $11,960 to $13,931 per resident in nominal dollars) is an accounting change, not new borrowing.",
    "Values are in 2025 dollars using the New York-area consumer price index, a measure of household prices, not of the costs the City borrows for; the Comptroller's nominal figures are kept in nominal_series.",
    "New York's debt covers functions that elsewhere belong to counties, school districts and some state agencies, so per-resident city debt is not directly comparable with other city governments."
  ],
